  "note": "What a Codex turn means, whatever the app. `exit` is the status the run was told to exit with; it is informational, and a classification never depends on it except where a case says so (a run with no output and exit 0 is unreadable). `stderr` is the process's own error text. `<cmd200>` stands for the first 200 characters of the 100,000-character command in long-command.jsonl (\"cmd \" fifty times). Steps keep only the command, cut to 200 characters; never a file's content or an output. Text from different agent messages is joined by a blank line. A violation (an item type the level does not allow, a web search, an MCP call, a file change) stops the turn at that line: text, steps and finish are empty and the error is nm_level_exceeded. A turn that did not complete and matches no failure is an interrupted run (nm_codex_crashed). The thread id is the one field of thread.started (the recordings scrub its key to __ID_1__). `verified` is true where a recording backs the case.",
